## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to handle equipment with strong reflective/transparent surfaces, a realistic industrial setting, and on-device adaptation under tight latency budgets.

## Key Concepts
- Reflective/transparent object pose estimation
- Differentiable rendering for photometric consistency
- On-device 3-shot adaptation via compact adapters
- Synthetic+real data with material randomization
- Edge latency and robustness under glare

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch
function forward(frame) {
  const feats = backbone(frame);
  const seg = segHead(feats);
  const pose = poseHead(feats);
  const render = differentiableRenderer(pose, frame);
  return {seg, pose, loss: computeLoss(render, frame)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to varying refractive indices?
- How would you quantify uncertainty in pose under specular highlights?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a complete edge-CV pipeline: real-time monocular 3D pose with per-object velocity, occlusion robustness, and rapid on-device adaptation to unseen parts. Emphasizes practical data strategies and evaluation in an industrial setting.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time monocular 3D pose estimation
- Per-instance velocity prediction for planning
- On-device few-shot adaptation (5-shot)
- Occlusion handling and data fusion strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: forward pass with adaptation trigger
function forward(frame, exemplars){
  let features = backbone(frame);
  let detections = detector(features);
  let poses = poseHead(features, detections);
  let vel = velocityHead(features, detections, previous);
  if (exemplars) adapt(poses, exemplars);
  return {detections, poses, vel};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify robustness to occlusion in your evaluation?
- What ablation would you run to isolate the velocity head contribution?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes on-device real-time hazard detection and 3D distance estimation under unseen categories, reflecting safety-critical constraints common in field deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth on edge
- On-device meta-learning (5-shot)
- Synthetic+real data with domain randomization
- Temporal tracking and occlusion robustness
- Evaluation: detection, depth error, latency

## Code Example

```javascript
function forward(img, backbone, detectorHead, depthHead) {
  const feats = backbone.extract(img);
  const boxes = detectorHead.predict(feats);
  const depth = depthHead.predict(feats);
  return { boxes, depth };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ablate depth head accuracy vs. detector latency?
- What failure modes dominate in cluttered sites, and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design real-time non-rigid 3D reconstruction from monocular input, integrating differentiable rendering and on-device adaptation—critical for AR try-on. It also probes data strategy, evaluation, and edge-latency trade-offs in a commercial retail context.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular non-rigid 3D reconstruction with deformable garments
- Differentiable rendering and photometric consistency
- Temporal fusion for stability
- 5-shot on-device adaptation for unseen fabrics
- Edge latency and data strategy (synthetic+real)

## Code Example
```javascript
function forward(frame, params){
  const latent = encoder(frame, params.enc);
  const mesh = deformableMesh(latent, params.shape);
  const render = renderer(mesh, params.tex);
  const loss = photometric(render, frame) + temporal(mesh);
  return {mesh, loss};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle occlusion or loose fabrics?
- How evaluate mesh quality vs. latency?
- How would you construct synthetic data for textiles?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests real-time monocular depth, multi-task learning, and pose estimation in a challenging forest domain, plus on-device few-shot adaptation and map fusion.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular depth and semantic/instance segmentation
- 6-DoF pose estimation for markers
- TSDF fusion for map building on edge hardware
- Differentiable rendering for domain adaptation
- On-device 5-shot adaptation with temporal consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-architecture sketch
class MonocularDroneNet {
  constructor(){ /* backbone */ }
  forward(frame){
    const depth = this.depthHead(frameFeatures)
    const segPose = this.segPoseHead(frameFeatures)
    const pose = this.markerPoseHead(segPose)
    const map = this.tsdfFuse(depth, pose)
    return {depth, segPose, pose, map}
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ablate temporal consistency vs per-frame independence?
- What data policies ensure robust marker generalization across weekly styles?
- How would you monitor edge latency under CPU/GPU contention?
